  faqHeading: "Cleaning company websites in the UAE: questions owners ask",
  faqs: [
    { question: "What does a cleaning company website in the UAE actually need?", answer: `Two separate booking paths, one for recurring service and one for one-off deep cleans, a visible price with its basis and minimum stated, a WhatsApp link with a prefilled message on every page, a tappable number in international format, your licence details, an explicit excluded-services list, and community pages only for areas you genuinely serve. Everything else is optional.` },
    { question: "How much does a cleaning company website cost in the UAE?", answer: `A focused site with both booking paths, eight to ten service pages and a handful of real community pages sits inside our static plan from ${P.site}. A full community-by-community build with internal linking designed in starts from ${P.seoSite}. A booking tool with real team availability is software rather than a website and starts from ${P.software}. Monthly search work starts from ${P.seo}.` },
    { question: "Should a cleaning company publish its prices online?", answer: "Yes. In this market a page with no price loses to a page with a higher price, because cost is the buyer's first question and an unanswered first question ends the visit. What matters is publishing the right basis per service, with the minimum hours, whether materials are included, whether the rate is per cleaner or per team, and the VAT position stated beside it." },
    { question: "Per hour or per maid: which pricing basis works better on a UAE cleaning website?", answer: "Per hour per cleaner is clearest for regular residential service and easiest for a buyer to compare, which is both its advantage and its risk. A per-maid visit block suits companies with a three or four hour minimum and moves the comparison away from pure hourly rate. Deep cleans should be fixed packages with an explicit exclusions list instead." },
    { question: "Can my website show real-time availability for cleaning bookings?", answer: `Only if something behind it knows your team roster, leave, travel time between communities and contract commitments. That is a rostering system and a software project from ${P.software}, not a website feature. For almost every cleaning company the honest design is request-and-confirm: the site collects a preferred window and promises a confirmation inside a stated time.` },
    { question: "Why does a deep clean quote need photographs?", answer: "Because condition, not size, sets the hours. Two identical apartments can be four hours apart depending on limescale, kitchen grease, AC grilles and leftover furniture, and a flat price from a dropdown means either losing money or renegotiating on the doorstep. Three to six phone photos let you send a firm price, and customers prefer a firm price to a fast guess." },
    { question: "Is WhatsApp really better than a booking form for a UAE cleaning company?", answer: "For most enquiries, yes. Buyers want to send a photo, ask one question and get a number, which is three exchanges rather than a form, and a voice note crosses language gaps a form cannot. Keep the form below it for corporate buyers who want a written trail. The important build detail is a prefilled message naming the service and area." },
    { question: "How many service-area pages should a cleaning company website have?", answer: "Only as many as you have genuine coverage and genuine detail for. Thirty real community pages, each carrying access, parking, building type and minimum-hours specifics, outperform three hundred pages that differ only by name. The test: remove the community name and read it again. If it could be about anywhere, a search engine will treat it that way too." },
    { question: "What trust signals should a UAE cleaning company put on its website?", answer: "Your registered trade name, licence number, authority and emirate; the municipal approvals your activity requires as confirmed by your licensing authority; liability insurance with a validity date; whether cleaners are your own employees; a written damage and complaint policy; and real photographs of your own team. Skip unverifiable job counters, unattributed award graphics and stock images of cleaners." },
    { question: "Is advertising maid services the same as holding a cleaning licence in the UAE?", answer: "Not necessarily, and this is worth checking before the wording goes live. Supplying domestic workers to households is a separately regulated activity administered through MoHRE-authorised Tadbeer centres, which is distinct from a commercial cleaning services licence. If your wording implies placement rather than cleaning delivered by your own staff, confirm with MoHRE or your licensing authority which activity you may market." },
    { question: "Does a UAE cleaning company website need an Arabic version?", answer: "It depends on your catchment. Residential demand in older Dubai districts, much of Sharjah and parts of Abu Dhabi searches in Arabic as often as English, and government or semi-government contract work usually expects it. A common path is launching in English, watching which enquiries arrive in Arabic, then adding the home page, the top services and the contact page as a priced phase." },
    { question: "Do you take photographs of our team and vans?", answer: "No. BtechWaleTech is three freelance developers in India with no UAE office, licence, phone number or staff, so we cannot shoot in the country, attend a site visit or meet a building manager. We will not substitute stock photographs and present them as your team either. Real photographs of your own crews are content you supply, and for a trust-led service they matter." },
  ],
